            "title": "Welcome: The Power of Verbs",
            "status": "uncompleted",
            "state": "active",
            "components": [
                {
                    "id": "heading-1-1",
                    "type": "heading",
                    "props": {
                        "content": "✨ Welcome to Verb Mastery: The Engines of Language",
                        "level": 1,
                        "align": "left"
                    },
                    "state": "active",
                    "status": "uncompleted"
                },
                {
                    "id": "paragraph-1-1",
                    "type": "paragraph",
                    "props": {
                        "content": "Every sentence you read, write, or speak needs a central engine to give it life and motion. That engine is the <strong>verb</strong>. Without verbs, ideas remain motionless fragments. In this lesson, you will explore the two fundamental types of verbs—<strong>Action Verbs</strong> and <strong>Stative Verbs</strong>—and master the golden rule of <strong>Subject-Verb Agreement</strong> to make your writing clear, precise, and grammatically accurate.",
                        "align": "left"
                    },
                    "state": "active",
                    "status": "uncompleted"
                },
                {
                    "id": "poll-1-1",
                    "type": "poll",
                    "props": {
                        "question": "Which type of word brings physical movement or action to a sentence?",
                        "options": [
                            {
                                "id": "opt1",
                                "text": "Action Verb (e.g. Sprinted, Launched)"
                            },
                            {
                                "id": "opt2",
                                "text": "Noun (e.g. Telescope, Planet)"
                            },
                            {
                                "id": "opt3",
                                "text": "Adjective (e.g. Bright, Ancient)"
                            },
                            {
                                "id": "opt4",
                                "text": "Preposition (e.g. Under, Through)"
                            }
                        ],
                        "points": 5,
                        "state": "active"
                    },
                    "state": "active",
                    "status": "uncompleted"
                }
            ]
        },
        {
            "id": "slide-2",
            "title": "Action Verbs: Physical & Observable Motion",
            "status": "uncompleted",
            "state": "active",
            "components": [
                {
                    "id": "heading-2-1",
                    "type": "heading",
                    "props": {
                        "content": "🏃 Action Verbs: Expressing Physical Movement",
                        "level": 2,
                        "align": "left"
                    },
                    "state": "active",
                    "status": "uncompleted"
                },
                {
                    "id": "paragraph-2-1",
                    "type": "paragraph",
                    "props": {
                        "content": "<strong>Action verbs</strong> (also called dynamic verbs) describe physical activities, movements, or processes that can be observed. Think of them as kinetic energy in writing. Examples include <em>run, leap, write, calculate, build,</em> and <em>whisper</em>.",
                        "align": "left"
                    },
                    "state": "active",
                    "status": "uncompleted"
                },
                {
                    "id": "paragraph-2-2",
                    "type": "paragraph",
                    "props": {
                        "content": "A key feature of action verbs is that they can easily take the <strong>continuous (-ing) tense</strong> to show an ongoing activity (for example: <em>'The scientist is building a prototype'</em> or <em>'The students were discussing the results'</em>).",
                        "align": "left"
                    },
                    "state": "active",
                    "status": "uncompleted"
                },
                {
                    "id": "flashcards-2-1",
                    "type": "flashcards",
                    "props": {
                        "title": "Action Verb Cards: Flip to see how action verbs create vivid pictures!",
                        "cards": [
                            {
                                "id": "fc1",
                                "front": "SWIFT MOVEMENT: Sprinted",
                                "back": "Action Verb: Describes high-speed physical running ('Maya sprinted across the field')."
                            },
                            {
                                "id": "fc2",
                                "front": "CREATIVE WORK: Crafted",
                                "back": "Action Verb: Describes physical or mental construction ('The artist crafted a sculpture')."
                            },
                            {
                                "id": "fc3",
                                "front": "SOUND & SPEECH: Whispered",
                                "back": "Action Verb: Describes vocal physical production ('Leo whispered the answer')."
                            }
                        ],
                        "mode": "practice",
                        "state": "active"
                    },
                    "state": "active",
                    "status": "uncompleted",
                    "mode": "practice"
                }
            ]
        },
        {
            "id": "slide-3",
            "title": "Stative Verbs: States of Being, Mind & Emotion",
            "status": "uncompleted",
            "state": "active",
            "components": [
                {
                    "id": "heading-3-1",
                    "type": "heading",
                    "props": {
                        "content": "🧠 Stative Verbs: Expressing Thoughts, Feelings & States",
                        "level": 2,
                        "align": "left"
                    },
                    "state": "active",
                    "status": "uncompleted"
                },
                {
                    "id": "paragraph-3-1",
                    "type": "paragraph",
                    "props": {
                        "content": "Unlike action verbs, <strong>stative verbs</strong> describe a state of being, an emotion, a mental thought, or a condition of possession. They express things that exist rather than physical actions you can watch someone do. Common stative verbs include <em>know, believe, understand, love, own, belong,</em> and <em>seem</em>.",
                        "align": "left"
                    },
                    "state": "active",
                    "status": "uncompleted"
                },
                {
                    "id": "paragraph-3-2",
                    "type": "paragraph",
                    "props": {
                        "content": "<strong>Important Rule</strong>: Stative verbs are rarely used in continuous (-ing) tenses. We say <em>'I understand the lesson'</em> (CORRECT), not <em>'I am understanding the lesson'</em> (INCORRECT).",
                        "align": "left"
                    },
                    "state": "active",
                    "status": "uncompleted"
                },
                {
                    "id": "matchingPairs-3-1",
                    "type": "matchingPairs",
                    "props": {
                        "title": "Match each stative verb to its correct category of meaning.",
                        "pairs": [
                            {
                                "id": "p1",
                                "left": "Believes / Remembers",
                                "right": "Mental Thought or Knowledge"
                            },
                            {
                                "id": "p2",
                                "left": "Admires / Loves",
                                "right": "Emotion or Feeling"
                            },
                            {
                                "id": "p3",
                                "left": "Owns / Possesses",
                                "right": "Possession or Ownership"
                            },
                            {
                                "id": "p4",
                                "left": "Appears / Seems",
                                "right": "State of Condition or Perception"
                            }
                        ],
                        "shuffled": true,
                        "points": 20,
                        "mode": "practice",
                        "state": "active"
                    },
