            "title": "Collective Nouns: Units vs. Individuals",
            "status": "uncompleted",
            "state": "active",
            "components": [
                {
                    "id": "heading-8-1",
                    "type": "heading",
                    "props": {
                        "content": "👨‍👩‍👧‍👦 Collective Nouns: Grouping as One",
                        "level": 2,
                        "align": "left"
                    },
                    "state": "active",
                    "status": "uncompleted"
                },
                {
                    "id": "paragraph-8-1",
                    "type": "paragraph",
                    "props": {
                        "content": "A <strong>collective noun</strong> names a group of people, animals, or items acting together as a single unit (for example: <em>team, class, jury, family, committee, squad</em>). In standard English, when the group acts as one unified body, the collective noun takes a <strong>singular verb</strong>.",
                        "align": "left"
                    },
                    "state": "active",
                    "status": "uncompleted"
                },
                {
                    "id": "paragraph-8-2",
                    "type": "paragraph",
                    "props": {
                        "content": "Example: <em>'The debate team <strong>prepares</strong> for the finals.'</em> ('Team' is treated as one single unit, so we use the singular verb 'prepares').",
                        "align": "left"
                    },
                    "state": "active",
                    "status": "uncompleted"
